---
import { OFFICIAL_SOURCES, SITE } from '../../config/site';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const FAQ_ITEMS = [
  {
    question: 'Is SEER2 lower than SEER for the exact same air conditioner?',
    answer:
      'Yes. Because the DOE M1 test procedure increases duct static pressure resistance, SEER2 is approximately SEER multiplied by 0.95 (about 4.5% to 5% lower), so SEER and SEER2 quotes cannot be compared directly without conversion.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does the federal 25C heat pump tax credit still apply in 2026?',
    answer:
      'No. Per IRS rules, the Section 25C Energy Efficient Home Improvement Credit (30% up to $2,000/year for heat pumps) applied only to systems placed in service on or before December 31, 2025, and expired for 2026 installations.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why does the calculator default yearly energy savings to $0?',
    answer:
      'Real cooling savings depend on run hours, local kWh rates, and duct leakage. Keeping savings at $0 unless you verify your own electric bills avoids inflating replacement ROI with brochure estimates.',
  },
] as const;

const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.question,
    acceptedAnswer: {
      '@type': 'Answer',
      text: item.answer,
    },
  })),
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="SEER2 Explained: DOE Minimums & 2026 Rebates | HVAC Second Opinion"
  description="Verified DOE 10 CFR 430.32 SEER2 minimums, ENERGY STAR heat pump ratings, SEER vs SEER2 conversion math, and why expired 25C credits shift focus to DSIRE."
  structuredData={faqSchema}
>
  <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Calculator</a> / <a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / <span>SEER2 Explained</span>
  </nav>

  <article class="prose">
    <h1>SEER2 Explained: What Efficiency Ratings Actually Save You</h1>
    <p class="guide-meta">
      Last updated: <time datetime={SITE.lastUpdated}>November 24, 2026</time> · Checked against primary federal sources
    </p>
    <p class="intro-lead">
      Contractors frequently justify a $3,000 higher bid by promising your power bill will drop in half. Before paying extra for top-tier SEER2 badges—or counting utility savings inside our <a href="/">calculator</a>—check the federal rules and real kilowatt-hour math below.
    </p>

    <h2>Verified DOE Regional Minimums (10 CFR 430.32)</h2>
    <p>
      Effective <strong>January 1, 2023</strong>, the U.S. Department of Energy (<a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">10 CFR 430.32</a>) established regional minimum standards under the <strong>SEER2 and HSPF2</strong> test procedures, applying higher external static pressure to simulate household ductwork:
    </p>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="DOE and ENERGY STAR SEER2 minimums" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Equipment Category</th>
            <th>Region</th>
            <th>Minimum Rating</th>
            <th>Authority</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Split-system central AC</th>
            <td>North</td>
            <td class="tabular">13.4 SEER2</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE (10 CFR 430.32)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Split-system central AC (&lt; 45,000 Btu/h)</th>
            <td>Southeast / Southwest</td>
            <td class="tabular">14.3 SEER2</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE (10 CFR 430.32)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Split-system central AC (≥ 45,000 Btu/h)</th>
            <td>Southeast / Southwest</td>
            <td class="tabular">13.8 SEER2</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE (10 CFR 430.32)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Split-system heat pump</th>
            <td>Nationwide</td>
            <td class="tabular">14.3 SEER2 / 7.5 HSPF2</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.doeSeer2} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DOE (10 CFR 430.32)</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">ENERGY STAR split heat pump</th>
            <td>Nationwide</td>
            <td class="tabular">15.2 SEER2 / 7.8 HSPF2</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.energyStar} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ENERGY STAR v6.1</a></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <figure class="manual-figure">
      <img
        src="/images/fig-05-seer2-regional-map.svg"
        alt="Comparison chart of DOE 10 CFR 430.32 regional SEER2 minimums alongside annual 3-ton cooling costs in mild Northern vs hot Southern climates."
        width="820"
        height="330"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Figure 05: Verified DOE 10 CFR 430.32 regional minimums vs. real annual cooling electricity cost across Northern and Southern run-hour profiles.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Why SEER and SEER2 Are Not Directly Comparable</h2>
    <p>
      Under the DOE M1 test procedure, testing equipment against realistic external duct static pressure reduces numeric scores for the exact same hardware: <strong>SEER2 ≈ SEER × 0.95</strong> (roughly 4.5% to 5% lower). For instance, a legacy 14.0 SEER condenser translates roughly to <strong>13.4 SEER2</strong>, and a 15.0 SEER unit translates roughly to <strong>14.3 SEER2</strong>. Never compare an older SEER brochure directly against a post-2023 SEER2 quote without converting scales—and demand an official AHRI Certified Reference Number in <a href="/guides/how-to-read-hvac-quote/">your written quote</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How to Estimate Your Real Electric Savings From Bills</h2>
    <p>
      Instead of accepting generic percentage claims from a sales pitch, spend three minutes calculating a realistic annual cooling budget directly from your utility history:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Find your shoulder-month baseline:</strong> Check your utility statements from mild spring or fall months (such as April and October) when neither air conditioning nor electric resistance heating runs heavily.</li>
      <li><strong>Isolate summer cooling usage:</strong> Subtract that shoulder-month kWh average from your peak cooling months (June through September) and sum those incremental cooling kilowatt-hours for the year.</li>
      <li><strong>Multiply by rate and efficiency ratio:</strong> Multiply seasonal cooling kWh by your electric rate ($/kWh), then multiply by <code>1 − (Old Effective SEER2 ÷ New SEER2)</code>. For instance, shifting $600 of annual cooling electricity from 10 SEER2 to 14.3 SEER2 trims roughly 30%, or ~$180/year.</li>
      <li><strong>Stay conservative when uncertain:</strong> If your bills mix pool pumps, EV charging, or varied occupancy, leave expected savings at <strong>$0</strong> inside the <a href="/">calculator</a> so hypothetical savings never mask an inflated replacement quote.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>2026 Tax Credits vs. Active State &amp; Utility Rebates</h2>
    <div class="source-callout">
      <strong>IRS Section 25C Notice:</strong> Per <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.irs25c} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS guidance</a>, the federal Section 25C credit (30% up to $2,000/yr for qualifying heat pumps) applied only to property <strong>placed in service on or before December 31, 2025</strong> and expired for 2026 installations. Verify local programs on <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.dsire} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DSIRE</a> before sizing incentives into a bid.
    </div>

    <div tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="2026 HVAC tax credit and rebate pathways" class="table-wrapper">
      <table class="estimate-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Incentive Track</th>
            <th>2026 Status</th>
            <th>Verification Rule</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Federal IRS Section 25C Credit</th>
            <td>Expired Dec 31, 2025 ($0 for 2026 installs)</td>
            <td><a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.irs25c} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IRS Section 25C</a></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Electric Utility Heat Pump Rebates</th>
            <td>Active in many utility territories</td>
            <td>Search ZIP on <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.dsire} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DSIRE</a>; often requires ≥15.2 SEER2 / 7.8 HSPF2 <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.energyStar} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ENERGY STAR</a> AHRI match</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">State Energy Office Rebates (HOMES/HEAR)</th>
            <td>State-by-state rollout schedules</td>
            <td>Confirm income/efficiency caps and pre-approval rules on <a href={OFFICIAL_SOURCES.dsire} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">DSIRE</a> before install</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Manufacturer / Distributor Rebates</th>
            <td>Seasonal cash discounts</td>
            <td>Compare pre-rebate net cost against baseline bids (see <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">contractor bid checklist</a>)</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Worked Example: Real Cooling Savings From SEER2 Upgrades</h2>
    <div class="worked-example">
      <p>
        Replacing an aging 3-ton (36,000 Btu/h) unit running near 10 SEER2 (common with older <a href="/guides/r22-refrigerant/">R-22 systems</a>) with a <strong>14.3 SEER2</strong> baseline unit vs. an <strong>18.0 SEER2</strong> variable-speed tier:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mild Northern climate (600 cooling hrs/yr, $0.16/kWh):</strong> 10 SEER2 uses <code>2,160 kWh = $346/yr</code>; 14.3 SEER2 drops to <code>1,510 kWh = $242/yr</code> (saves <strong>$104/yr</strong>). Moving from 14.3 to 18.0 SEER2 saves only an extra <strong>$50/yr</strong>—requiring 40 years to repay a $2,000 price jump.</li>
        <li><strong>Hot Southern climate (1,800 cooling hrs/yr, $0.15/kWh):</strong> 10 SEER2 uses <code>6,480 kWh = $972/yr</code>; 14.3 SEER2 drops to <code>4,531 kWh = $680/yr</code> (saves <strong>$292/yr</strong>); 18.0 SEER2 trims another <strong>$140/yr</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        Plug conservative summer-bill estimates into Check D of the <a href="/">calculator</a> alongside <a href="/guides/5000-rule/">the $5,000 and 50% rules</a>, or leave savings at $0 when bills are unknown. Also ask your <a href="/guides/how-to-vet-hvac-contractor/">vetted contractor</a> to measure duct static pressure so leaky ducts never erase nameplate efficiency.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-list">
      {FAQ_ITEMS.map((item) => (
        <details>
          <summary>{item.question}</summary>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">{item.answer}</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      ))}
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
